<section>
  <h2 class="sec-head">What the port deletes</h2>
  <p class="prose">This is the part worth naming plainly: most of what makes AndeSim's driver complicated exists only because the legs are separate processes. FastSim's mechanism does not need any of it. Of <b>7,883</b> production lines in <code>driver/</code>, these <b>2,054</b> go away &mdash; removed, not rewritten.</p>

  <div class="sites">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>File</th><th>Lines</th><th>Why it exists today</th><th>Replaced by</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>run_plan.cpp</code> + <code>.hpp</code></td><td>652</td><td>Spawn each leg in order, thread the state file forward, collect mcycle/minstret</td><td>one <code>.lua</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>engine_registry.cpp</code> + <code>.hpp</code></td><td>379</td><td>argv templates per engine and per role ("fast-leg", "accurate-leg")</td><td>Lua module params</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>shm.cpp</code> + <code>.hpp</code></td><td>363</td><td>Allocate a shared mmap file, resolve a tier, cross-check sizes</td><td>one <code>gs_memory</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>process.cpp</code> + <code>.hpp</code></td><td>320</td><td>fork/exec, capture stdout, map exit codes</td><td><code>sc_start()</code> + CCI params</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>memmap.cpp</code> + <code>.hpp</code></td><td>254</td><td>Compose QEMU's and vsim's memory-map views and check they agree</td><td>one <code>router</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>plan.hpp</code></td><td>86</td><td>The phase-plan grammar: steps, roles, triggers</td><td>one <code>.lua</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="prose">Their tests go with them. The <code>hybrid_state_v1</code> record survives as a struct &mdash; it is still a contract between two separately built objects &mdash; but the file it was written to does not.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="sec-head">What stays</h2>
  <div class="out">
    <div><b>The CLI.</b> <code>run</code>, <code>profile</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>doctor</code> and 28 options. Something has to translate them into Lua and CCI parameters &mdash; that translator is new code, and it is the price of keeping the user experience unchanged.</div>
    <div><b>The measurement logic.</b> <code>elf_syms.cpp</code> resolves markers to PCs, <code>state_diff.cpp</code> backs <code>--verify</code>, <code>profile.cpp</code> drives profiling. None of it cares how the engines are launched.</div>
    <div><b>Everything the user touches.</b> The runtime library, <code>andesim/ROI.h</code>, the container image, the per-engine vsim builds.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="sec-head">The order</h2>
  <p class="prose">Cycle mode is the first mode that can work, and it is a real milestone rather than a checkpoint: it needs only one new module, and it proves the hardest mechanical change before anything depends on it.</p>

  <div class="stages">

    <div class="stage">
      <div class="stage-rail">
        <div class="stage-id">S0</div>
        <div class="stage-cost">small &middot; andesim only</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stage-body">
        <h3>Pin the number before anything moves</h3>
        <p>The cycle count is the product, and any change under the core can move it silently. Record <code>kernel_cycles</code> for a set of fixtures as a golden baseline first. Write down the fidelity contract in the two halves people confuse: the <em>bytes</em> of guest RAM may move to <code>gs_memory</code>, but the <em>response latency</em> reaching the core must not change &mdash; and that latency is not produced by the RTL, <code>SimpleMemory</code> adds it <span class="ref">(vsim src/platform/simple_memory.hpp:62)</span>.</p>
        <div class="gate"><b>Done when</b> the baseline test fails if a fixture's cycle count moves by one cycle.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="stage">
      <div class="stage-rail">
        <div class="stage-id">S1</div>
        <div class="stage-cost">large &middot; the hard one</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stage-body">
        <h3>VsimCore becomes a FastSim module &rarr; <b>cycle mode runs</b></h3>
        <p>Wrap the Verilated core as a <code>moduletype</code> that binds to the <code>router</code>. The pin-to-TLM adapter already exists <span class="ref">(vsim src/utils/axi_to_tlm_bridge.hpp)</span>, so the work is not the bus &mdash; it is <b>who owns <code>sc_start</code></b>.</p>
        <p>Today vsim steps the kernel itself from <code>sc_main</code>: <code>reset()</code> at <span class="ref">main.cpp:577</span>, the run loop at <span class="ref">simulator.hpp:749</span>, and every DMI transaction at <span class="ref">systemc_dmi_bus.hpp:41</span>. FastSim calls <code>sc_start()</code> once and lets the kernel run. <code>sc_start</code> cannot be called from inside the kernel, so all three sites move into an <code>SC_THREAD</code> and become <code>wait()</code>.</p>
        <p>Two things fall out for free once that inversion is done: an <code>SC_THREAD</code> can <code>wait()</code> on an event, which is what a handoff doorbell needs; and <code>QemuCpu::reset_cb</code> requires an <code>SC_THREAD</code> caller anyway <span class="ref">(fastsim models/qemu_components/include/cpu.h)</span>.</p>
        <div class="gate"><b>Done when</b> <code>andesim --mode cycle</code> produces the same cycle count as today, driven by a Lua platform.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="stage">
      <div class="stage-rail">
        <div class="stage-id">S2</div>
        <div class="stage-cost">medium &middot; fixes the fd bug</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stage-body">
        <h3>One device set &rarr; <b>fast mode runs</b></h3>
        <p>Assemble the shared platform on <code>-M none</code>, starting from <code>conf/v2_linux_ae350_none.lua</code>, which already carries the whole AE350 board as SystemC modules and boots Linux. Register vsim's <code>SimControl</code>, <code>SMU</code> and <code>UART</code> as moduletypes and bind them to the router; port <code>guest_va_to_host()</code> to <code>gs_memory</code> DMI.</p>
        <p>Take <code>-semihosting</code> out of <code>qemu_args</code>. Semihosting is a magic instruction sequence the <em>simulator</em> recognises &mdash; a Verilated netlist cannot, on principle. The MMIO mailbox is the only channel both CPUs have, so it is the one the shared platform keeps.</p>
        <div class="gate"><b>Done when</b> <code>andesim --mode fast</code> runs on the Lua platform, and a file the guest opened is still readable after a mode switch.</div>
        <p class="drops"><b>Deletes:</b> the second implementation of 18 syscalls patched into QEMU's <code>andes_ae350.c</code>, and the guest-fd bug &mdash; not by transporting the fd table, but by there being one process and one table.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="stage">
      <div class="stage-rail">
        <div class="stage-id">S3</div>
        <div class="stage-cost">medium &middot; needs new libqemu-cxx</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stage-body">
        <h3>State exchange &rarr; <b>hybrid mode runs</b></h3>
        <p>Memory needs no transport: both CPUs address the same <code>gs_memory</code>. Registers still do, and this does <em>not</em> get simpler &mdash; a Verilated netlist has no register setter, so the debug module stays the only architectural write port and the whole DMI resume path survives intact.</p>
        <p>Getting registers <em>out of</em> QEMU is the new work. Two blockers, below. Sequencing follows a pattern already in the tree: an MMIO write from the QEMU side, <code>gs::async_event::async_notify()</code>, an <code>SC_THREAD</code> waking on the kernel thread &mdash; exactly how <code>StopperV2</code> and <code>QemuInstance</code>'s guest-exit work.</p>
        <div class="gate"><b>Done when</b> <code>--verify</code> still matches the QEMU oracle, and the hybrid cycle count matches the recorded S0 baseline.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="stage">
      <div class="stage-rail">
        <div class="stage-id">S4</div>
        <div class="stage-cost">medium &middot; the payoff</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stage-body">
        <h3>CLI over Lua, and the deletion</h3>
        <p>Translate the 28 options into Lua parameters and CCI presets, then remove the six files above. The exit path has a ready-made pattern: FastSim's <code>multi_arch_toplevel.cpp</code> calls <code>sc_start()</code> once and then scans broker parameters for <code>.failed</code> and <code>.guest_exit_status</code> to build the process exit code.</p>
        <div class="gate"><b>Done when</b> every AndeSim command behaves as it does today, and <code>driver/</code> no longer spawns a process.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="sec-head">Blockers found in the source</h2>
  <div class="risks">

    <div class="risk">
      <h3>TCG plugins are compiled out</h3>
      <p>AndeSim drains state with a TCG plugin, but FastSim's QEMU cannot load one. <code>meson_options.txt:84</code> defaults <code>plugins</code> to false, and <code>configure:248</code> takes it from <code>default_feature</code>, which <code>--without-default-features</code> in <span class="ref">fastsim CMakeLists.txt:139</span> sets to no. The plugin does not port as-is.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="risk">
      <h3><code>qemu::Cpu</code> has no register access</h3>
      <p>It exposes <code>halt</code>, <code>reset</code>, <code>set_soft_stopped</code>, <code>async_safe_run</code> and <code>as_rw</code> for the CPU's address space &mdash; and nothing for GPRs or CSRs. Prefer adding an accessor to libqemu-cxx over re-enabling the whole plugin subsystem: <code>as_rw</code> is precedent, added by Andes for exactly this class of need.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="risk">
      <h3>The RAM alias bases disagree</h3>
      <p>vsim puts the uncached alias at <code>0x8_0000_0000</code> <span class="ref">(memory_map.h)</span>; QEMU's AE350 puts <code>UNCACHEABLE_ALIAS</code> at <code>0x1_0000_0000</code>. Two separate maps hide this today; one router will not. A wrong alias corrupts data silently instead of faulting &mdash; reconcile it in S2, before anything runs on the shared map.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="risk">
      <h3>PLIC and PLMT freeze while the RTL runs</h3>
      <p>They are the only AE350 devices with no SystemC implementation anywhere, and the wrapped versions keep their state inside QEMU. Halting the QEMU CPU stops its quantum keeper, so <code>mtime</code> does not advance for the whole measured region. Fine for a bare-metal region that reads no timer; not fine for a Linux one. Port PLMT when a Linux region is actually needed &mdash; it is small.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="risk">
      <h3>Two engines in one process share symbols</h3>
      <p>Already hit once in FastSim: <code>STB_GNU_UNIQUE</code> binds vague-linkage statics process-wide even under <code>RTLD_LOCAL | RTLD_DEEPBIND</code>, and <code>tools/ace/rebind_unique_symbols.py</code> rebinds 232 symbols per simulator to work around it. If AndeSim ever loads more than one engine build at once, this is waiting.</p>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="sec-head">Still open</h2>
  <div class="decision">
    <h3>Must the accurate engine always be the RTL?</h3>
    <p>FastSim already ships a per-instruction cycle model (<code>libacefsim_rv64.so</code>, <code>riscvhf::CycleModel</code>). Where that accuracy is enough, those cases work today and never need S1 at all. Where the product is RTL-derived confidence, S1 stands. This changes the plan's size, not its shape &mdash; but it decides how urgent S1 is.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="decision">
    <h3>Which vsim engine builds must coexist?</h3>
    <p>Today each engine is a separate process, so the question never came up. One process makes it real, and the answer sets whether the <code>STB_GNU_UNIQUE</code> workaround becomes part of AndeSim rather than a FastSim curiosity.</p>
  </div>
</section>

